+### Read-only SQLite history (Pakka downstream patch)
+
+The Node SQLite export also provides `openNodeSqliteReader(file, { prefix? })`. It opens an existing file
+read-only and verifies its schema without initialization, migration, writer ownership, a Session, or task
+recovery. The writer APIs above keep their existing behavior.
+
+```typescript
+import { BACKGROUND_CONTEXT } from "@earendil-works/chord/context";
+import { openNodeSqliteReader } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable/storage/sqlite/node";
+
+const context = BACKGROUND_CONTEXT;
+const reader = await openNodeSqliteReader("./session.sqlite");
+try {
+	const page = await reader.snapshot(async (read) => {
+		const conversations = await read.scanConversations({}, 32, undefined, context);
+		const conversation = conversations.items[0];
+		const entries = conversation
+			? await read.entryArchive.scan({ conversationId: conversation.id }, 64, undefined, context)
+			: undefined;
+		return { sequence: read.sequence, conversations, entries };
+	}, context);
+} finally {
+	await reader.close(context);
+}
+```
+
+The borrowed view offers `conversation`, `scanConversations`, `entryArchive.header/scan/read`,
+`findDocument`, and `document`, using existing native record types. Document reads materialize stored
+bases/deltas without running application migration callbacks; saved `running` state does not prove that
+a task is still executing. Header pages contain at most 256 records and body ranges at most 64 KiB.
+
+A callback observes one committed snapshot, including WAL data. Await all its reads, do not hold it across
+network or UI waits, and do not call the reader itself from inside it. Its view expires when the callback
+settles. Pages are separate snapshots; fix the scope/range and use Entry `commitSeq` when following up.
+The reader does not promise reconstruction of arbitrary old mutable states.
+
+Missing files and unsupported schemas are refused rather than created or migrated.
+`SqliteReaderVersionError` (from `/storage/sqlite`) identifies the supported and observed schema versions.
+Close rejects new snapshots, drains admitted callbacks and closes without a checkpoint or truncation.
+Cancellation affects only the read. Read-only SQLite may participate in WAL coordination-file I/O; a
+changing source is never opened as `immutable`.
+
+The downstream build compiles the Context-free SQLite companion from the same fixed Chord source.
+The public Chord runtime remains shared; a second complete Chord package is not embedded.
+
 The package root loads TypeBox, because the tool task validates arguments with pi-ai's `validateToolArguments()`. That costs about 23 MB of peak RSS unbundled, about 4 MB in a tree-shaken bundle.
 
